<p>Genjikai Karate</p>
<p>The name “Genjikai is taken from the name of a famous literary character, Genji, noted for his flawless courtesy and aesthetic genius.  Sensei Nishida’s family heritage is also associated with Genji’s historical clan, and thus the name has personal significance for him.  “Kai” means organization or association.  Genjikai might be said to refer to an association of people who train in the way of karate according to the ideals embodied by the character Genji, that is, courteously and with disciplined attention to the correctness of form.  This being the first step to the perfection and harmony of body, mind, and spirit.</p>
<p>Karate Do means the way of the empty hand.  It is martial arts form that is characterized by strength, power and diligence.  It came to Japan by way of Okinawa and China.  Karate Do is a way of fighting without weapons by striking an opponent with hands and feet.  Fists, toes, elbows, and knife-hands become weapons.  One practices and perfects strikes and blocks.  By training the body to move, breathe, and act properly the mind and spirit are strengthened.  Karate training begins with achieving a healthy body.</p>
<p>Genjikai Karate Do will also offer the practices of Tai Chi and Qi Gong.  Tai Chi is martial arts with its roots in China.  Its movements are structured, smooth, even, slow, and continuous.  The practice promotes calmness, lightness, slowness, exactness and perseverance.  It can be practiced for health purposes, as a defense system, or a way to focus and calm the mind.</p>
<p>Qi Gong, spelled Chi Kung or Qi Gung, is an ancient Chinese art of increasing the internal chi energy within the body.  It is the development of an internal power to improve health, maintain youthfulness and perfect mental powers.  It is called an internal aerobics.  It is said to build the immune system, balance hormones, eliminate toxins, strengthen the bone structure, improve circulation, and open the joints.</p>
<p>Cardio-kickboxing will also be part of the program.  It is aerobics with kicks and punches.  Martial arts skills will be developed while keeping rhythm with music.</p>
<p>All these forms will increase alertness and body awareness, enhance concentration and sensitivity.  The body will develop strength, flexibility, endurance, and quickness.  As self-discipline emerges, self-confidence will escalate and a respect of self and others will emerge.</p>
<p>Genjikai Karate Do will offer ways to develop mindfulness.  With intention, attention, and presence students are able to grow in body, mind, and spirit to become healthy, happy human beings.</p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<p>Biography of Makio Nishida</p>
<p>A native of Tokyo, Japan, Makio Nishida began his training at seven years of age in a school that taught Okinawan Gojuru Karate.  He also studied Shotokan Karate, but by the time he was fifteen years old he was junio black belt in Masutatsu Oyama'’ Kyokushin-kai.  Oyama’s Kyokushin style of karate is the largest single influence on the Genjikai style, and Oyamas’s style itself is derived from Okinawa Goju-Ryu, Shotokan, Uechi-Ryu, and Chinese Kempo.  Sensei Nishida has also studies Aikido, Kendo, Taikiken, kickboxing, western boxing, Tai Chi and Qi Gong.</p>
<p>Makio Nishida came to the United States first in 1970.  He formed Japan Karate Do Genjikai in 1978 in Santa Fe, New Mexico, but moved To Texas in 1983.  He opened his dojo in Arlington in 1986 after teaching at the Caravan of Dreams where he was the General Manager at its inception.  He later taught at Euless Recreational Center.  He is affiliated with the Texas Board of Professional Arts Instructors, and he received the 7th Dan rank in 1989.  He has enjoyed a large measure of competitive success in New Mexico, Colorado, and Texas with an accumulation of over 300 trophies.  In 1994 Sensei Nishida took 2nd in Kubodo and 4th in kata in Nationals in AAU tournament.  Currently, he is an AAU judge and coach.</p>
<p>Sensei Nishida has owned and operated Japan Karate Do Genjikai in Arlington for 12 years.  He recently has closed that dojo and opened a new school Genjikai Karate in Fort Worth.  The classes offered are karate, tai chi, qi gong, cardio kickboxing, and rape awareness/self defense.</p>

